Body:
The difference in the error response could be the Opencode version. With 1.17.18, I see the same response as @Dayclone : 
> Bad Request: {"detail":"The 'gpt-5.6-sol' model is not supported when using Codex with a ChatGPT account."}

The original report was against an older version (1.17.15), which might not have the same logic, especially if everything needed about the new models (e.g., Oauth connectivity) does not live on Models.dev.

Confirming this on opencode 1.17.15 (linuxbrew binary) against NVIDIA's hosted NIM endpoint (`integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1`, model `nvidia/nemotron-3-content-safety`). The endpoint rejects any request carrying tools with vLLM's `"auto" tool choice requires --enable-auto-tool-choice and --tool-call-parser to be set`, and `tool_call: false` in the model config does not stop opencode from sending them.

Independent confirmation of the root cause described above: grepping the bundled binary shows `capabilities.toolcall` is computed during config/catalog merge (`toolcall:f.tool_call??_?.capabilities.toolcall??!0`) but never read anywhere in the request path, so the config field is a no-op for outgoing requests.

Working user-level workaround until this is fixed: define an agent with the legacy `tools` wildcard map, which does prevent tool definitions from being sent:

```json
{
  "agent": {
    "safety": {
      "mode": "primary",
      "model": "nvidia/nvidia/nemotron-3-content-safety",
      "tools": { "*": false }
    }
  }
}
```

Verified working end-to-end with `opencode run --agent safety` -- the request goes out with no tools and the endpoint responds normally.

Note for anyone trying the recommended `permission` field instead: `permission: { "...": "deny" }` does not help here. It blocks tool execution but the tool definitions are still included in the request body, so providers that reject tool-bearing requests still fail.

